R('b1-q2','a','pool-config,single-node','Pool size; Minimum Idle Instances',
 'Instance pools retain reusable cloud instances, reducing cold provisioning delay when a job cluster starts. This helps a scheduled report whose elapsed refresh includes cluster startup, not the report logic itself.',
 'Separate startup latency from reproducibility, tests, permissions, and source control.',
 'A report that must refresh quickly benefits when its job cluster can obtain already available pool instances. A pool targets provisioning time rather than changing the query result.',
 'Reproducibility depends on controlled code, runtime, and inputs. Keeping instances idle does not version or freeze those dependencies.',
 'Tests and data quality rules detect incorrect behavior. A pool supplies compute instances and does not run correctness assertions on the report.',
 'Git folders manage branches and commits across collaborators. Instance reuse does not provide version control.',
 'Who can run a report is controlled by permissions. Pool membership does not grant every stakeholder access to the report or underlying data.')
R('b1-q7',None,'vacuum,history','Caveats for vacuum; Configure data retention',
 'VACUUM can physically remove obsolete data files and prevent time travel, but the default file retention is seven days. A three-day-old restore failure needs an unstated shorter retention or other deletion, so the provided scenario is underspecified.',
 'Check both log retention and data-file retention; never infer a three-day retention policy from default VACUUM behavior.',
 'VACUUM is the listed operation that physically removes eligible obsolete files. However, a normal seven-day retention would not explain deletion of files needed for this three-day-old version; additional configuration is required.',
 'Time travel reads an earlier snapshot and does not delete its files. It needs both the transaction history and referenced data files to remain available.',
 'DELETE HISTORY is not the documented Delta maintenance command for removing old physical data files. Do not substitute an invented command for VACUUM.',
 'OPTIMIZE rewrites active files for layout and compaction while retaining old files for concurrent readers and history. Physical cleanup is subsequently governed by retention and VACUUM.',
 'DESCRIBE HISTORY reports commits rather than removing data. Inspecting history cannot by itself explain missing physical files.',status='underspecified-retention')
R('b1-q8',None,'git','Clone a repo; Commit and push changes; Merge branches',
 'Current Databricks Git folders support all five listed operations, including merging. The old Merge key is outdated; no operation in these choices must universally be done outside Databricks.',
 'Treat old Repos limitations as version-sensitive, and check current Git folders capabilities.',
 'Commit is available in the Git operations dialog. It records selected local changes, so it is not an outside-only operation.',
 'Pull is available in the dialog to obtain remote changes. It is not restricted to an external Git client.',
 'Commit and push are supported together in the workspace workflow. Publishing local commits does not require leaving Databricks in general.',
 'The UI can clone a remote repository into a Git folder. Cloning is therefore not an operation that must be external.',
 'The current documentation includes Merge branches and conflict resolution. The source Merge answer reflects an obsolete restriction, not the present platform.',status='outdated-no-valid-option')
R('b1-q9','b','delta,lakehouse','What is Delta Lake in Databricks?; How does the Databricks lakehouse work?',
 'Delta adds ACID transactions and schema enforcement to lake storage. These improve reliable, consistent updates and validation rather than merely broadening supported workloads.',
 'For data quality and reliability, look for transaction guarantees and enforced schemas.',
 'Handling structured and unstructured data describes broad storage support. Traditional lakes can already hold both, so this does not isolate the quality improvement.',
 'ACID-compliant transactions keep writes atomic and reads consistent as data changes. The lakehouse documentation identifies Delta transactional storage and schema enforcement as core improvements.',
 'SQL makes data accessible to analysts but does not itself make incomplete or conflicting writes transactional. A query interface is distinct from a quality guarantee.',
 'Open formats improve interoperability and reduce dependence on one engine. Openness alone does not validate data or enforce atomic updates.',
 'ML and AI workloads describe consumers of stored data. Supporting those workloads does not by itself prevent unreliable records or inconsistent table updates.')
R('b1-q10','b','git,notebooks','Create a new branch; Switch to a different branch',
 'Git folders add branch-based project development backed by a remote Git repository. Notebook revision history is not a substitute for separate collaborative feature branches.',
 'Branch isolation is the distinctive source-control advantage in this comparison.',
 'Notebook editing already maintains revision history, so automatic preservation of edits is not the distinguishing Git benefit. Git instead makes explicit commits to branches.',
 'Git folders support creating and switching branches. This allows independent feature development before merging changes into a shared branch.',
 'Reverting a notebook revision is not unique to Git folders. The question asks for a differentiating advantage rather than a capability shared by both workflows.',
 'Commit messages and change context can help review, but the documented branch workflow is the direct distinguishing feature. Comments alone do not provide isolated project histories.',
 'Git folders track a remote repository and its branches rather than replacing the Git provider entirely. The source wording is truncated across pages; the original PDF remains authoritative.')
