R('b1-q11',None,'legacy-acl,ownership','Manage privileges on objects in the Hive metastore; Manage object ownership',
 'For legacy Hive metastore table ACLs, a workspace administrator can transfer an absent owner’s table. Unity Catalog also permits metastore admins, eligible parent owners, and MANAGE holders, so the unqualified word must makes the source answer conditional.',
 'Distinguish legacy workspace table ACLs from Unity Catalog ownership authority.',
 'A commercial account representative is not automatically a data-object administrator. An organizational contact role does not confer table ownership privileges.',
 'Ownership transfer is supported by ALTER ... OWNER TO and Catalog Explorer. The owner leaving does not make transfer impossible.',
 'Workspace admin is the expected answer in a legacy table-ACL context. It is not a universal exclusive authority for Unity Catalog tables, which is why the question remains conditional.',
 'Being the lead engineer does not automatically grant transfer privileges. The new engineer needs an applicable administrative, ownership, or MANAGE authority.',
 'An existing owner can normally transfer ownership, but this person no longer has access. Recovery must use another authorized principal rather than require the departed owner.',status='metastore-context-missing')
R('b1-q12','e','dataframes,notebooks','Read a table into a DataFrame; SQL syntax highlighting and autocomplete in Python commands',
 'Spark SQL and PySpark operate over the same registered tables. spark.table("sales") returns a DataFrame for Python tests; SQL query text must instead be passed to spark.sql.',
 'Use a table reader for a table name and spark.sql for a complete SQL query.',
 'SELECT is valid SQL syntax but this bare text is not the requested Python command. Wrap a full SELECT query in spark.sql when using it in a Python cell.',
 'Databricks exposes tables through SQL and DataFrame APIs. There is no separate SQL-only physical copy that prevents Python access.',
 'spark.sql expects a SQL statement rather than just the identifier sales. spark.sql("SELECT * FROM sales") would be a valid alternative, but it is not the printed option.',
 'spark.delta.table is not the documented SparkSession table reader. Delta storage is read through standard table/DataFrame APIs.',
 'spark.table("sales") loads the registered table as a DataFrame. Python assertions can then inspect the same records used by the SQL team.')
R('b1-q13','c','describe-schema','Syntax; Examples',
 'DESCRIBE SCHEMA returns metadata including the filesystem location; DATABASE is an interchangeable synonym. It inspects rather than changes the schema.',
 'A request to report a location calls for DESCRIBE, not USE, ALTER, or DROP.',
 'DESCRIBE LOCATION is not the documented schema-inspection syntax. The object type is DATABASE or SCHEMA, followed by its name.',
 'DROP DATABASE removes the schema rather than reporting its location. It is a destructive operation and does not answer the inspection request.',
 'DESCRIBE DATABASE customer360 returns the schema name, comment, and location. The official reference explicitly treats DATABASE and SCHEMA as synonyms.',
 'ALTER changes metadata rather than reporting it, and a DBPROPERTIES string is not a location-inspection command. This option also has malformed punctuation.',
 'USE DATABASE changes name resolution for subsequent statements. It does not display the schema’s storage metadata.')
R('b1-q14','d','create-table','Parameters — COMMENT; AS query',
 'CREATE TABLE accepts a COMMENT string that documents the table. It is metadata for discoverability, not a security control or automatic PII classifier.',
 'Distinguish documentation metadata from governance controls that actually restrict PII access.',
 'A table can carry a COMMENT and other supported metadata. The platform does not prevent documenting that a table contains PII.',
 'Quoting COMMENT PII makes it a string literal rather than a COMMENT clause. SQL needs the clause keyword outside the quoted description.',
 'TBLPROPERTIES requires parenthesized key/value pairs, not a bare PII token. A valid property could be used in another statement, but this fragment is malformed.',
 'COMMENT "Contains PII" is the supported metadata clause among the choices. The source’s missing blank must be placed in the CREATE TABLE clauses before AS SELECT; the comment alone does not enforce privacy.',
 'PII by itself is not a CREATE TABLE clause. Meaningful human labels still need valid SQL metadata syntax.')
R('b1-q15','d','filter-function,auto-schema','filter function — Arguments; Schema inference',
 'Array functions manipulate nested collection values, including arrays loaded from JSON. For example, filter(array, lambda) retains selected elements within each row rather than filtering entire table rows.',
 'Arrays are nested values; windows and table-level orchestration solve different problems.',
 'An array has an element type; array functions are not a general license to mix arbitrary unrelated types. Structures can contain typed fields, but that is different from unconstrained heterogeneous values.',
 'Partitions and windows organize computations over groups of rows. Array functions operate on collection elements inside a value.',
 'Time intervals are handled by timestamp, date, and window operations. Array APIs are not specifically a time-interval execution mechanism.',
 'JSON can contain nested arrays and structures, and array functions transform those elements directly. The filter reference demonstrates an ARRAY argument plus an element lambda.',
 'A Spark array is a column value, not a procedural list of catalog tables. Automating table jobs requires orchestration or explicit program logic.')
R('b1-q16','c','merge','Data deduplication when writing into Delta tables',
 'MERGE matches incoming rows to target keys and can insert only unmatched records. The incoming batch must also be deduplicated; MERGE alone is not a universal uniqueness constraint.',
 'Look for key-based matching when avoiding repeated inserts, and separately consider duplicates within the source.',
 'DROP removes a table or object rather than merging records safely. It cannot append new unique data while preserving the target.',
 'IGNORE is not the standalone Delta write operation that matches incoming business keys. Ignoring an existing object or error is not record-level deduplication.',
 'MERGE can use WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT so previously present keys are not inserted again. The documentation separately warns that duplicates inside the incoming batch need deduplication.',
 'APPEND adds incoming rows without automatically comparing them to existing keys. Replaying a batch can therefore create duplicate records.',
 'INSERT INTO normally appends rows and does not enforce uniqueness of business keys. MERGE supplies the matching condition missing from this choice.')
R('b1-q17',None,'sql-function','Syntax; RETURNS data_type',
 'Current SQL scalar-function syntax permits an omitted return type when it can be derived from the expression. Both A and D use CREATE FUNCTION and RETURN CASE, so the question does not have a unique answer under current documentation.',
 'RETURNS declares a result type; RETURN introduces the expression. A return-type declaration can now be optional for a SQL scalar function.',
 'A uses CREATE FUNCTION, a declared STRING result, and RETURN CASE. This is a documented form, assuming the runtime’s quoted-string rules permit the shown literals.',
 'CREATE UDF is not the documented creation keyword, and this option also lacks RETURN before CASE. Use CREATE FUNCTION with a valid body instead.',
 'Changing the body to RETURN CASE does not make CREATE UDF valid. The function declaration still uses the wrong keyword.',
 'D has CREATE FUNCTION and a RETURN expression with a derivable result type. Current documentation makes RETURNS data_type optional for SQL scalar functions, so it cannot be rejected solely for omitting it.',
 'E supplies a result type and RETURN body, but still begins with CREATE UDF. The declaration keyword remains invalid.',status='multiple-valid-current-version')
R('b1-q18','a','filter-function','Syntax; Arguments; Returns',
 'FILTER(employees, i -> i.years_exp > 5) applies the predicate to each array element and returns an array of retained employees. The result preserves the array’s element type.',
 'Look for the input array first and a lambda parameter bound to each element second.',
 'A supplies the employees array and a lambda whose parameter i refers to the current employee. The years_exp predicate keeps only employees above the stated threshold.',
 'B refers to exp_employees as input even though that is the new output being defined, and it omits a valid lambda. It cannot filter the existing employees array as requested.',
 'C supplies a Boolean expression without binding an array element through a lambda. The function expects filter(array, function), not a free years_exp column predicate.',
 'D uses a CASE expression on the array rather than filtering its individual employees. It would not construct the required subset array element by element.',
 'E uses the output alias exp_employees as the filter input before that output exists. The lambda form alone cannot correct the wrong input array.')
R('b1-q19','b','notebooks,python-udf,job-schedules','Mix languages; Python function examples; Schedule jobs',
 'A Python notebook can issue SQL through Spark and apply ordinary Python control flow around selected statements. Schedule the notebook daily, then gate only the final query by the date condition.',
 'Keep the daily schedule separate from the conditional execution of one statement.',
 'The platform already supports Python orchestration of SQL, so a feature request is unnecessary. Notebook language support is not restricted to one rigid SQL program.',
 'Wrapping SQL execution in Python allows a weekday check to guard the final query. Earlier statements can still run on every daily execution.',
 'Running the entire program only on Sundays would skip required daily queries. It changes the schedule of every statement instead of only the last one.',
 'Changing access permissions is a security operation rather than ordinary program control flow. It would also affect other consumers of the table.',
 'Separating tables changes the data model but does not by itself decide when a query runs. The requirement concerns execution logic, not storage layout.')
R('b1-q20','c','copy','Get started using COPY INTO to load data',
 'COPY INTO is retriable and idempotent by default: previously loaded source files are skipped. Re-running it does not imply appending the same files again.',
 'When a repeated COPY INTO adds no rows, check file ingestion history before assuming a refresh or file-format error.',
 'FILEFORMAT specifies Parquet; FORMAT_OPTIONS is for optional format settings. The absence of FORMAT_OPTIONS does not prevent all Parquet ingestion.',
 'COPY INTO can discover source files from a location without an explicit FILES list. File selection options are not a mandatory list of every filename.',
 'An already loaded file is skipped by the normal idempotent behavior. That explains an unchanged target count on a repeated run without assuming a syntax failure.',
 'Parquet is explicitly a supported COPY INTO source format. Its use is not a reason the command cannot ingest records.',
 'A refresh command is not the mechanism that makes a committed COPY visible in the Delta table. Skipped input files explain why no new rows were written.')
R('b1-q21',None,'jdbc','Read data with JDBC; Python and SQL examples',
 'The current JDBC examples use the jdbc data source with URL and dbtable options. None of the printed choices is that documented short provider name; the legacy fully qualified string in the key is not independently established by the retrieved reference.',
 'Separate the Spark JDBC source name from the database driver class and connection URL.',
 'The key’s org.apache.spark.sql.jdbc string is not the provider form demonstrated in the current documentation. Without a version-specific reference establishing this class alias, it remains unverified rather than being silently accepted.',
 'Auto Loader discovers files in cloud storage, not SQLite tables through a JDBC URL. It does not implement the connection in the question.',
 'DELTA describes transactional file-based table storage. It is not the source provider that opens an external SQLite database connection.',
 'sqlite identifies a database family but is not the documented Spark jdbc provider option. A SQLite JDBC driver and a valid JDBC source are separate requirements.',
 'org.apache.spark.sql.sqlite is not the documented JDBC source. Naming an assumed provider class does not replace the JDBC connector.',status='provider-not-established')
R('b1-q22',None,'set-ops,create-table','UNION ALL or DISTINCT; AS query',
 'UNION is the intended operation to combine compatible rows and remove duplicates, but every printed query omits the SELECT projection. Preserve this source defect rather than treating the malformed B statement as executable SQL.',
 'Check syntax as well as the intended operation: a correct conceptual operator does not repair missing query expressions.',
 'INNER JOIN combines matching row pairs and requires a valid query and join structure. It is not a vertical concatenation of March and April transactions.',
 'UNION is conceptually appropriate and removes duplicate rows by default. As printed, SELECT FROM supplies no projection; a supported repair would be SELECT * FROM each table, but that repair is not in the original choice.',
 'An outer join combines rows across related columns, not a simple union of the two months. This option is also syntactically incomplete.',
 'INTERSECT returns rows common to both inputs. The question says there are no duplicates between months, so intersection does not produce all transactions.',
 'MERGE is a data-modification statement, not a set operator placed between these SELECT fragments. It does not supply the requested CTAS union syntax.',status='malformed-options')
R('b1-q23','d','notebooks,python-udf','Python notebook execution; Python function examples',
 'A Python if statement uses == for equality and and for Boolean conjunction. A Boolean variable can be tested directly instead of comparing it with the string "True".',
 'Distinguish assignment = from comparison ==, and a Boolean value from a quoted string.',
 'A uses assignment syntax where the if condition requires a comparison. It cannot test whether day_of_week equals 1 as written.',
 'B contains assignments inside the condition and compares to a quoted string rather than the Boolean value. Both syntax and intended value checks are wrong.',
 'C has the correct day comparison but tests review_period against the string "True". A Boolean True is not that string, so this would not implement the requirement.',
 'D combines day_of_week == 1 with the truth value of review_period. Both conditions must hold before the indented block runs.',
 'E combines invalid assignment punctuation with a bitwise operator. It is not the Boolean conjunction and equality test the scenario requires.')
R('b1-q24','e','notebooks,dataframes','SQL syntax highlighting and autocomplete in Python commands; Query a table',
 'spark.sql executes SQL query text and returns a DataFrame. The f-string interpolates the chosen table name before Spark receives the query; table names should come from trusted or validated input.',
 'A complete SELECT string goes to spark.sql, while spark.table takes an identifier rather than a query.',
 'spark.delta.sql is not the documented SparkSession query executor. Delta tables use the standard Spark SQL interface.',
 'spark.delta.table is not the documented interface, and the supplied argument is a full SQL query rather than a table identifier. The query needs execution through spark.sql.',
 'spark.table accepts a table name, not the SELECT statement shown. Use it when loading a named relation directly.',
 'dbutils.sql is not the documented way to execute this query from Python. Databricks utilities do not replace the SparkSession SQL API.',
 'spark.sql receives the constructed SELECT text and returns query results as a DataFrame. It matches both the Python execution context and the complete query argument.')
R('b1-q25','c','drop-table','DROP TABLE; External table behavior',
 'Dropping an external table removes catalog metadata but leaves externally managed data files. File size is not the condition deciding ownership of the data lifecycle.',
 'External tables separate the catalog definition from the lifecycle of external files.',
 'There is no 10-GB cutoff in the documented DROP TABLE external-table rule. Size does not convert a managed table to external.',
 'Small table size likewise does not explain metadata-only deletion. The managed-versus-external distinction is the relevant condition.',
 'External table data is left in place when the table definition is dropped. This directly matches disappearing catalog metadata with retained source files.',
 'A table still has a data location; not explicitly writing LOCATION in SQL is not a general explanation for retained files. Storage ownership is determined by the table type.',
 'Managed table data is subject to platform lifecycle management when dropped, including delayed recovery/purge behavior in Unity Catalog. Metadata-only retention of external files is the external-table rule.')
R('b1-q26','e','create-table,views','CREATE TABLE; CREATE VIEW — TEMPORARY',
 'A persistent table materializes data and can be accessed across sessions with suitable privileges. An ordinary view stores a query definition rather than the requested physical result data.',
 'Cross-session use plus materialized data points to a persistent table, not a temporary view.',
 'A database or schema groups objects and is not itself the result relation created from two tables. It does not materialize that join or query result.',
 'A function defines reusable computation. It is not the persistent stored relation the question requests.',
 'An ordinary view can be shared across sessions but does not store a physical copy of its query result. That misses the materialization requirement.',
 'A temporary view is scoped to its creating session. It fails the explicit requirement that other engineers use the object from other sessions.',
 'A persistent table created from the query stores the result and can be queried in other sessions. This satisfies both durability and physical data requirements.')
R('b1-q27','d','expectations','What are expectations?; Expectation tracking metrics',
 'Pipeline expectations express row-level quality checks and report validation metrics. Delta Live Tables is now documented under Lakeflow pipelines, but the expectations concept maps to this source question.',
 'Monitoring row-rule violations points to pipeline expectations rather than file discovery or catalog browsing.',
 'Unity Catalog controls governance and access to data objects. Governance alone does not define the source record quality rules described here.',
 'Data Explorer browses objects and metadata. It is not the pipeline execution feature that evaluates each row against a declared expectation.',
 'Delta Lake provides transactional table storage and schema enforcement. The named managed-pipeline feature for richer expectation metrics is DLT/Lakeflow.',
 'DLT/Lakeflow expectations evaluate Boolean quality constraints and report retained or dropped invalid rows. They are directly intended for automated pipeline data quality monitoring.',
 'Auto Loader ingests newly arriving files and manages schema evolution. It is not the expectation framework for arbitrary business-quality checks across transformed tables.')
R('b1-q28','a','stream-checkpoint','Structured Streaming checkpoints',
 'Structured Streaming uses checkpoints and write-ahead logs to record offsets, committed batches, and recovery state. Replayable sources and idempotent sinks are complementary guarantees rather than the two progress-recording mechanisms asked for.',
 'If the question asks what records offset progress, look for checkpoint and log state, not only sink behavior.',
 'Checkpoint state includes per-batch source offsets and commits, working with write-ahead logs for recovery. That is the documented progress bookkeeping requested.',
 'The checkpoint documentation explicitly describes recorded source offsets. Structured Streaming can therefore resume from recorded progress.',
 'Replayable sources and idempotent sinks help achieve reliable end-to-end processing. They are not the two state-recording structures named in this question.',
 'An idempotent sink prevents repeated output effects, while a write-ahead log records progress. This pairing omits the checkpoint information explicitly requested.',
 'Checkpointing records progress, but sink idempotency is an output behavior rather than an offset log. The question asks for the two bookkeeping mechanisms together.')
R('b1-q30','e','medallion','Ingest raw data to the bronze layer',
 'Bronze preserves source fidelity in a table representation, with minimal validation and a schema describing stored fields. It is not the cleansing or aggregation layer.',
 'A schema on raw records does not imply that all values have been cleaned or normalized.',
 'Bronze is not defined by containing fewer records than source files. Preserving history and enabling reprocessing are central goals.',
 'Bronze does not become more truthful merely by landing data. It preserves raw fidelity so downstream stages can apply explicit quality logic.',
 'Aggregating source records is usually a Gold-oriented operation. Bronze retains the raw records needed for replay and auditing.',
 'A raw source has not been refined, so describing Bronze as less refined is not a meaningful documented relationship. Bronze organizes raw records without discarding their original meaning.',
 'A Bronze table stores raw records with table fields and types, potentially using strings or VARIANT to protect fidelity. Cleansing and deduplication are deferred to Silver.')
R('b1-q33',None,'auto-schema','How does Auto Loader schema inference work?',
 'Auto Loader deliberately infers JSON and CSV fields as STRING by default to avoid type conflicts; cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes=true changes that behavior. None of the options accurately states that configurable policy.',
 'A default inference policy is not a permanent limitation: distinguish STRING defaults from support for typed data.',
 'The prompt says no explicit schema was supplied, so a mismatch between a supplied schema and inferred schema is not established. The behavior is explained by the default inference policy.',
 'JSON is textual, but that alone does not prevent typed Boolean or numeric inference. The documented configurable STRING default, omitted here, is the technical reason.',
 'Auto Loader supports typed schemas and can infer column types when configured. Saying it only works with strings is false and makes the supplied C key unreliable.',
 'Nothing in the question establishes a null in every field. Default STRING inference does not depend on all columns containing nulls.',
 'Auto Loader supports schema inference and evolution. The existence of a specific default type policy contradicts the assertion that it cannot infer schemas.',status='no-accurate-option')
save()
